            },
            "privateNotes": "",
            "publicNotes": "<p>While one oceanid queen may rule over an entire inland sea, the majority define their territories in the vast, wide-open ocean by the flow of currents, the edges of continental shelves, and the curves of recurring weather patterns. As such, the boundaries between oceanid queendoms are both literally and figuratively fluid, making them difficult for land-born creatures to comprehend. Wise sailors know to avoid offending any oceanid queen, as those who do are forced to retire lest they accidentally stumble into the domain of the ruler they insulted, who will not hesitate to capsize entire fleets in revenge.</p><hr /><p>Where naiads are bound to their freshwater springs and nereids walk as they will between wave and shore, oceanid nymphs are, to their cores, creatures of the seas. They ride the open waves as naturally as dolphins and live among the deepest depths without care for cold, darkness, or pressure. As such, they're a rare and mysterious sight for those mortals who dwell on land but well-known to denizens of the sea, such as merfolk, for whom they're often confused.</p>\n<p>Oceanids can be as tempestuous as their watery home, with moods that change on a whim. They're quick to love and even quicker to anger. At heart, however, they're nurturing guardians, especially to the young, the elderly, the injured, and the desperate. It's not unheard of for oceanids to take in and raise children who've been abandoned near the shore, and sailors who speak of them with respect often miraculously survive storms and sinkings that otherwise leave no survivors.</p>",
            "publication": {
                "license": "ORC",
                "remaster": true,
                "title": "Pathfinder Feybound"
            }
        },
